%% Answer plans for every past T1 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T1QA.

**2016 Q2 — Status of Women's Studies in Pakistan; your views on autonomy/integration.** Define WS (1969–70; "academic arm of the movement"). Origins in Pakistan: Shirkat Gah 1975, WAF 1981, Aurat Foundation 1986, Mumtaz and Shaheed 1987. Institutions: five centres 1989; degrees; journals; CSS 2016. Achievements: research → laws of 2010/2016; NCSW staff; NPDEW 2002. Problems table (funding, faculty, curriculum, data, resistance, ghetto). Debate: Bowles and Klein 1983; autonomy vs integration; ghettoisation vs dilution. View: dual strategy — autonomy as engine, integration as road; answer the "Western agenda" charge through local integration. Conclude: arrived but fragile.

**2016 Q6 — Theories of social construction of gender.** Sex vs gender; Beauvoir; essentialism vs constructionism. Seven theories, each thinker + mechanism + Pakistani example: psychoanalytic (Chodorow), social learning (Bandura, Mischel), cognitive (Kohlberg's three stages), schema (Bem 1981), doing gender (Goffman; West and Zimmerman), performativity (Butler), structural (Lorber, Connell, Risman). Summary table. Agents. Critique (body, agency, universals). Interactionist conclusion: made, so remakeable.

**2017 Q2 — Define and discuss Gender Studies; differentiate WS and GS.** Scott 1986 in the introduction. Definition card (construction, power, multidisciplinary, intersection); discipline features (committed, reflexive, intersectional). Scope (seven areas); aims (five). Why it emerged (Gilligan, Waring, movement, "add and stir"). Significance (CEDAW 1996, SDG 5; Pakistan data). WS (Cornell, SDSU, NWSA). Seven-criteria table; causes of the shift; examples (honour killing; maternal health); similarities; Modleski; partners.

**2017 Q7 — Comprehensive note on autonomy vs integration.** Where should gender knowledge live? Background: rapid growth, insecure position, Bowles and Klein 1983. Autonomy defined + six arguments; integration defined + six arguments. Risks table. Hospital analogy. Dual strategy. Legacy: feminist epistemology (Harding 1986), institutions, curriculum transformation, Beijing twin track. Pakistan: strong autonomy (1989), weak integration; local integration answers "Western agenda". Debate never ends — rebalance in good and bad funding years.

**2018 Q2 — Fundamental differences GS vs WS with examples; status of WS in Pakistan.** WS origin and aims; GS origin (Oakley, Rubin, Scott, Connell, queer theory). Six fundamental differences as sub-headings, each with an example (subject, concept, politics, standpoint, theory, institutions). One-problem-two-lenses table (honour killing, maternal health, politics). Why the difference is fundamental (documentation vs system). Common ground; Modleski. Status: history, institutions, achievements, challenges, way forward.

**2018 Q3 — Essay: sex vs gender, reflecting nature vs nurture.** Terms and stakes. Origins: Mill 1869, Beauvoir 1949, Money 1955, Stoller 1968, Oakley 1972. Why feminists needed it ("biology is not destiny"). Nature: hormones, CAH, averages, sociobiology. Nurture: Mead, history, Baby X, socialisation, Fine and Joel. Reimer. Is sex constructed? (Laqueur, intersex range, Butler; critics). Ortner. Interactionism. Pakistan (literacy, judges, pilots, CM) and policy. Conclusion.

**2019 Q2 — Differentiate GS from WS; trace historically the need for GS; scope and significance; Pakistan.** Difference table. Four dated phases of need: androcentric knowledge (pre-1960s); the movement creates WS (1969–70s; *Towards Equality* 1974); limits of WS (gender concept, men's studies, queer theory, intersectionality); policy demand (GAD; Beijing 1995). Flow diagram. Scope (seven areas, widening). Significance (academic, policy, social). Pakistan throughout: 1989 centres; gap report 2026; LFPR; laws; CSS 2016; Beijing, CEDAW 1996 and the gender machinery created demand.

**2020 Q2 — Deconstruct "gender".** Derrida's binaries. Eight meanings, each with a thinker: grammatical (genus; Urdu), synonym for sex, roles (Oakley), identity (Stoller), performance (West and Zimmerman; Butler), institution/structure (Lorber; Risman), relation of power (Scott), policy category. Onion analogy; one girl's life shows all layers. Binary table; hierarchical, not natural (androgyny), unstable (women engineers; Sufi care). Constructed, so reconstructable.

**2020 Q4 — "Language is gendered": implication and examples.** Implies: reflects, reproduces, can change. Theory: Sapir–Whorf; Lakoff 1975; Spender 1980. Forms with English and Urdu examples: generic masculine; marked terms; semantic derogation (Schulz); insults and idioms; naming; violence in words; conversation (Zimmerman and West; Tannen). Effects table. Change: inclusive terms; new words; Pakistani laws 2010, 2016, 2018; "Aurat March". Caution: not sufficient alone. Wording of laws and notices matters for civil servants.

**2021 Q2 — How the autonomy/integration debate contributed to the field.** Debate in brief (Bowles and Klein). Then contributions as headings: identity and aims; feminist epistemology (Harding; standpoint); institutions (NWSA 1977); curriculum transformation; interdisciplinarity (care economy, intersectionality); model for mainstreaming (Beijing twin track); reflexivity (move to GS). Flow diagram. Pakistan (1989; NCSW alongside ministry gender units). Limits (rivalry, tokenism, donors). Built its own house and renovated others.

**2021 Q3 — Masculinity and femininity: natural or social?** Define; "ancient" ≠ natural (slavery, caste). Biological view (testosterone; Wilson). Psychoanalytic (Freud; Chodorow). Learning and cognitive ("*mard ko dard nahi hota*"). Connell card (four types; patriarchal dividend; *joru ka ghulam*). Performative. Mead. Historical change. Many "traditions" recent (colonial codification; *chador aur chaar-diwari*). Pakistani change. Verdict table: interactionist — form and ranking social.

**2022 Q2 — Multidisciplinary nature in detail.** Multi vs inter vs trans. Four reasons. One heading per discipline with thinker and Pakistani example (sociology, anthropology, psychology, history, economics, political science and law, biology and medicine, literature/linguistics/media, development and religious studies). Child-marriage example. Strengths–challenges table; link to the autonomy debate. Summary conclusion.

**2023 Q2 — Status of Gender Studies in Pakistan; views on autonomy vs integration.** Near repeat of 2016: say "Gender Studies" and show the evolution. SWOT: strengths (degrees, research, professionals, policy impact incl. Islamabad DV law 2025); weaknesses (money, people, theory, data, ghetto); opportunities (CEDAW, SDG 5, demand, the 2026 gap report, digital); threats (backlash, budgets, co-option). SWOT table. Debate. View: concrete integration plan by discipline (table). Answer the tokenism objection.

**2023 Q3 — Differentiate GS and WS; multidisciplinary nature in detail.** One-third to the table (with the honour-killing row), two-thirds to disciplines (grid + bullets with dates). Pakistani illustration: 24.4% LFPR needs six disciplines. Interdisciplinary blending (care work; medical evidence in rape trials). Link to the autonomy debate. Multidisciplinarity is the field's method, not a weakness.

**2023 Q4 — Define masculinity and femininity; where traditional roles come from.** Thesis: biological start, social history. Definition cards; Connell; emphasised femininity; *sharif aurat*, *izzat*. Six sources, each evidenced: biology; economy (Boserup, Alesina et al.); property (Engels); religion and culture; law, state, colonialism; socialisation and media. Flow diagram. Sources reinforce each other. Pakistan: change and persistence (LFPR 24.4%). Roles chosen vs imposed.

**2024 Q8(a) — Short note: is sex also a social construct?** (~600 words.) Oakley's distinction; problematizing. Yes: Laqueur; intersex range (Sax 0.018%, Fausto-Sterling 1.7%); medical and sports decisions; Butler. Boundaries drawn by people. No: binary reproduction; most fit; classification ≠ creation; real consequences; Nussbaum. *Khuntha* box. Both sides agree on dignity for intersex people and khawaja siras. Conclusion: biological fact interpreted through social categories.

**2026 Q2 — Constructionism and queer theory; the transgender debate and the 2018 Act.** Constructionism (Beauvoir, Oakley, Berger and Luckmann) and queer theory (Foucault, Butler, Sedgwick, de Lauretis; Laqueur, Fausto-Sterling). Critique (body, abstraction, Western, tension with feminism). Context: Mughal roles; 1871; SC 2009; Census 2017 (10,418). Act 2018 (ss. 3, 7). 2022 controversy (both sides). FSC 19 May 2023. Table: argument → theory → place in debate. Activism; appeal pending; Art. 203D. Balanced view on Arts 14, 25, 227. Agreement already exists on dignity and safety.

